    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is completely understandable to feel disappointed and to look for explanations after investing significant effort into reaching the final stage of a leadership role. \n\nHowever, to answer your questions directly: **on its own, the fact that the panel was made up entirely of women is not evidence of bias, nor is it a reliable sign that the process was compromised.** \n\nHere is an objective breakdown of how selection panels, leadership criteria, and post-interview processes typically work in engineering consultancies, and how you should weigh this situation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Does an all-female panel indicate bias?\nIn professional and legal contexts, the demographic composition of a panel alone does not constitute evidence of discrimination. \n* Just as an all-male panel does not automatically prove bias against female candidates, an all-female panel does not inherently disadvantage male candidates.\n* In mid-to-large consultancies, interview panels are usually composed of people holding specific roles (e.g., the Managing Director, the Practice Lead, an HR/People Director, or a related Discipline Head). Their presence is almost always dictated by their position or availability rather than an effort to curate a single-gender panel.\n* Suggesting or pursuing a claim of bias based purely on the panel’s gender balance would be legally very difficult to substantiate and carries severe reputational risks within the industry.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \"15 years vs. 8 years\" and the nature of a \"Head of Group\" role\nIt is common for candidates with more years of experience to be puzzled when passed over for someone with fewer years. However, for a **Head of Group** position:\n* **Commercial and client focus over technical seniority:** Both of you possess a chartered licence (the requisite professional baseline). While 15 years and major project delivery demonstrate high technical competence, a \"Head of Group\" is predominantly an executive and commercial role. Consultancies prioritize client retention, business development, team leadership, and winning tenders.\n* **\"Excellent client relationships\":** If the successful candidate has demonstrated an exceptional ability to bring in work, nurture high-value client accounts, and navigate complex stakeholder environments, consultancies will often weigh that more heavily than additional years of technical design experience.\n* **8 years chartered:** Eight years of post-qualification/industry experience is typically sufficient to understand delivery risks while demonstrating the energy and trajectory needed for department leadership.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The two-week delay for feedback\nWaiting two weeks for written feedback can feel agonizing, but it is standard practice in many corporate environments:\n* **Contract negotiations:** Consultancies rarely provide detailed rejection feedback to shortlisted runners-up until the preferred candidate has formally signed their contract and cleared any background checks.\n* **HR / Legal sign-off:** Written feedback for senior leadership appointments is often vetted by HR to ensure consistency, which introduces bureaucratic lag.\n* **Workload:** The panel members likely have heavy day-to-day operational responsibilities.\n\nA two-week silence is not a sign of evasion or foul play; it is simply standard corporate latency.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a polite, professional follow-up:**\n   After two weeks, it is entirely reasonable to send a brief, courteous note:\n   > *\"I wanted to follow up on my request for feedback regarding the Head of Structural Engineering interview. I am keen to understand areas where I can strengthen my profile for future leadership opportunities. I would welcome a brief written summary or a 10-minute call at your convenience.\"*\n   *(Offering a short phone call often yields more candid, actionable feedback than a formal written document.)*\n\n2. **Frame the feedback around executive competencies:**\n   When you do speak with them, avoid comparing your CV directly to the announcement. Instead, ask targeted questions:\n   * *How did my vision for the commercial growth of the group align with the board's expectations?*\n   * *Were there specific leadership or client-facing areas where other candidates stood out?*\n\n3. **Avoid raising the gender composition:**\n   Unless you have tangible, documentary evidence of discriminatory remarks or procedural irregularities, bringing up the panel’s gender will almost certainly be perceived as defensive, damage professional relationships, and close the door to future opportunities with that firm or that network. Focus strictly on qualifications, interview performance, and commercial strategy.\n",
